The solution has two paths, depending on whether the exact product fits in a 64-bit integer.

1. **Small products:** multiply exactly with integers. If no overflow occurs, strip the trailing zeros and count them as `C`. If at most 10 digits remain, print them as they are. Otherwise print the first 5 digits, `...`, the last 5 digits, then `e` and `C`.

2. **Large products:** the product overflows, so it has far more than 10 digits after trailing zeros are removed. Then:
   - Count the factors of 2 and 5 across all numbers in the range. Then `C = min(c2, c5)`.
   - The last 5 digits come from multiplying modulo 10^5. Strip every factor of 2 and 5 from each number first. Then multiply back in `2^(c2-C)` and `5^(c5-C)`. This gives the product divided by 10^C, taken mod 10^5.
   - The first 5 digits come from the sum of `log10(i)` over the range, minus `C`. Take the fractional part `f` of that sum and compute `floor(10^(f+4))`.

```php
class Solution {

    /**
     * @param Integer $left
     * @param Integer $right
     * @return String
     */
    function abbreviateProduct($left, $right) {
        // exact attempt
        $p = 1;
        $overflow = false;
        for ($i = $left; $i <= $right; $i++) {
            if ($p > intdiv(PHP_INT_MAX, $i)) {
                $overflow = true;
                break;
            }
            $p *= $i;
        }
        if (!$overflow) {
            $c = 0;
            while ($p % 10 == 0) {
                $p = intdiv($p, 10);
                $c++;
            }
            $s = (string)$p;
            if (strlen($s) <= 10) {
                return $s . "e" . $c;
            }
            return substr($s, 0, 5) . "..." . substr($s, -5) . "e" . $c;
        }
        $c2 = 0;
        $c5 = 0;
        $logSum = 0.0;
        $mod = 100000;
        $m = 1;
        for ($i = $left; $i <= $right; $i++) {
            $logSum += log10($i);
            $x = $i;
            while ($x % 2 == 0) {
                $x = intdiv($x, 2);
                $c2++;
            }
            while ($x % 5 == 0) {
                $x = intdiv($x, 5);
                $c5++;
            }
            $m = ($m * ($x % $mod)) % $mod;
        }
        $C = min($c2, $c5);
        for ($k = 0; $k < $c2 - $C; $k++) {
            $m = ($m * 2) % $mod;
        }
        for ($k = 0; $k < $c5 - $C; $k++) {
            $m = ($m * 5) % $mod;
        }
        $logP = $logSum - $C;
        $frac = $logP - floor($logP);
        $first = (int)floor(pow(10, $frac + 4) + 1e-9);
        if ($first >= 100000) $first = 99999;
        return $first . "..." . str_pad((string)$m, 5, "0", STR_PAD_LEFT) . "e" . $C;
    }
}
```

