The examples include trailing spaces in the strings (e.g. "cbd "). A space sorts before the lowercase letters, so a naive minimum-character scan would pick the space. To avoid that, `f` only considers characters from 'a' to 'z' and ignores everything else.

Word lengths are at most 10, so f(W) lies between 1 and 10. The plan:

1. Compute f for each word and build a count array `cnt[1..10]` of how many words have each frequency.
2. Build suffix sums so that `suf[k]` is the number of words with f > k.
3. For each query, compute f(q) and append `suf[f(q)]` to the answer.

To compute f(s), count occurrences of each letter in one pass, then take the count of the smallest letter that appears at all. I use an array of size 26 and skip any character outside 'a'..'z'.

I make the count array of size 12 so indexing stays safe. `suf[k] = sum over j > k of cnt[j]`, built from 10 down to 0. If a trimmed string has length 10, f is at most 10, so `suf[10] = 0` and nothing goes out of range.

```php
class Solution {

    /**
     * @param String[] $queries
     * @param String[] $words
     * @return Integer[]
     */
    function numSmallerByFrequency($queries, $words) {
        $f = function($s) {
            $c = array_fill(0, 26, 0);
            $n = strlen($s);
            for ($i = 0; $i < $n; $i++) {
                $o = ord($s[$i]) - 97;
                if ($o >= 0 && $o < 26) $c[$o]++;
            }
            for ($i = 0; $i < 26; $i++) {
                if ($c[$i] > 0) return $c[$i];
            }
            return 0;
        };
        $cnt = array_fill(0, 13, 0);
        foreach ($words as $w) {
            $cnt[$f($w)]++;
        }
        $suf = array_fill(0, 14, 0);
        for ($k = 12; $k >= 0; $k--) {
            $suf[$k] = $suf[$k + 1] + ($k + 1 <= 12 ? $cnt[$k + 1] : 0);
        }
        $res = [];
        foreach ($queries as $q) {
            $res[] = $suf[$f($q)];
        }
        return $res;
    }
}
```

