Two strings are close exactly when they have the same length, use the same set of characters, and have the same multiset of character frequencies.

1. If the lengths differ, return false.
2. Count the frequency of each letter (26 slots) in both strings.
3. For every letter, check that it appears in both strings or in neither. If one string has it and the other doesn't, return false.
4. Sort both frequency arrays and compare them. If they are equal, return true.

The examples in the statement contain trailing spaces (e.g. "abc "). The real inputs are lowercase letters only, so I count only the characters a–z.

```php
class Solution {

    /**
     * @param String $word1
     * @param String $word2
     * @return Boolean
     */
    function closeStrings($word1, $word2) {
        if (strlen($word1) != strlen($word2)) return false;
        $a = array_fill(0, 26, 0);
        $b = array_fill(0, 26, 0);
        $n = strlen($word1);
        for ($i = 0; $i < $n; $i++) {
            $a[ord($word1[$i]) - 97]++;
            $b[ord($word2[$i]) - 97]++;
        }
        for ($i = 0; $i < 26; $i++) {
            if (($a[$i] == 0) != ($b[$i] == 0)) return false;
        }
        sort($a);
        sort($b);
        return $a === $b;
    }
}
```

