Greedy two-pointer approach: keep `lo = 0` and `hi = n`. For each character, if it is `'I'`, output `lo` and increment it; if it is `'D'`, output `hi` and decrement it. Whichever value remains at the end (`lo == hi`) is appended as the last element.

Why this works: after an `'I'`, the next value is always larger than `lo`, since everything left is at least `lo + 1`. After a `'D'`, the next value is always smaller than `hi`, since everything left is at most `hi - 1`.

