For each index `j` where `nums[j] == key`, every index in the range `[j-k, j+k]` (clipped to the array bounds) is k-distant. Scanning left to right and keeping a pointer to the next index not yet added keeps the output sorted and free of duplicates.

1. Keep `$next = 0`, the smallest index not yet added to the result.
2. For each `j` with `nums[j] == key`, set `start = max($next, j - k)` and `end = min(n - 1, j + k)`.
3. Add every index from `start` to `end` to the result, then set `$next = end + 1`.

Because `j` increases, each new range starts at or after the previous one, so no index is added twice.

```php
class Solution {

    /**
     * @param Integer[] $nums
     * @param Integer $key
     * @param Integer $k
     * @return Integer[]
     */
    function findKDistantIndices($nums, $key, $k) {
        $n = count($nums);
        $res = [];
        $next = 0;
        for ($j = 0; $j < $n; $j++) {
            if ($nums[$j] == $key) {
                $start = max($next, $j - $k);
                $end = min($n - 1, $j + $k);
                for ($i = $start; $i <= $end; $i++) {
                    $res[] = $i;
                }
                if ($end + 1 > $next) $next = $end + 1;
            }
        }
        return $res;
    }
}
