I'll use a reverse-graph topological sort (Kahn's algorithm). Each node's out-degree is the length of its adjacency list. Terminal nodes have out-degree 0 and are safe, so they start the queue. For each node popped from the queue, I decrement the out-degree of every node that has an edge into it. A node whose out-degree reaches 0 is safe and joins the queue. Sorting the safe nodes at the end gives ascending order.

Steps:
1. Set `outdeg[i] = count(graph[i])` and build `rev[j][] = i` for each edge i→j.
2. Queue every node with out-degree 0.
3. Pop a node, mark it safe, and decrement `outdeg` for each predecessor in `rev`. Queue any predecessor that reaches 0.
4. Collect the safe nodes in index order, which is already ascending.

```php
class Solution {

    /**
     * @param Integer[][] $graph
     * @return Integer[]
     */
    function eventualSafeNodes($graph) {
        $n = count($graph);
        $outdeg = array_fill(0, $n, 0);
        $rev = array_fill(0, $n, []);
        for ($i = 0; $i < $n; $i++) {
            $outdeg[$i] = count($graph[$i]);
            foreach ($graph[$i] as $j) {
                $rev[$j][] = $i;
            }
        }
        $queue = [];
        for ($i = 0; $i < $n; $i++) {
            if ($outdeg[$i] == 0) $queue[] = $i;
        }
        $safe = array_fill(0, $n, false);
        $head = 0;
        while ($head < count($queue)) {
            $u = $queue[$head++];
            $safe[$u] = true;
            foreach ($rev[$u] as $p) {
                $outdeg[$p]--;
                if ($outdeg[$p] == 0) $queue[] = $p;
            }
        }
        $res = [];
        for ($i = 0; $i < $n; $i++) {
            if ($safe[$i]) $res[] = $i;
        }
        return $res;
    }
}
