Approach: for each stone, keep the set of jump sizes that can land on it. Process the stones in order and propagate forward.

- Map each stone position to an index. The set for stone 0 starts as {0}, so the first jump (k-1, k, k+1 with k = 0) can only be 1 once we drop jumps of size 0.
- For each stone, for each k in its set, try jumps k-1, k, k+1. Skip any jump that is ≤ 0. If the landing position is a stone, add that jump size to the landing stone's set.
- Return true if the last stone's set is non-empty.

Both examples work out as expected. In example 2, the gap from 4 to 8 is too large, so stone 8 gets no jump sizes and the answer is false.

Complexity is O(n²) time and space with n ≤ 2000. Each set holds at most about n distinct values, so about 4M entries in the worst case, which is acceptable. Sets are stored as PHP arrays keyed by jump size.

An early exit applies when stones[1] != 1, since the first jump must be 1. The DP already handles this, because stone 0 only offers jump 1.

```php
class Solution {

    /**
     * @param Integer[] $stones
     * @return Boolean
     */
    function canCross($stones) {
        $n = count($stones);
        $idx = [];
        foreach ($stones as $i => $s) {
            $idx[$s] = $i;
        }
        $sets = array_fill(0, $n, []);
        $sets[0][0] = true;
        for ($i = 0; $i < $n; $i++) {
            if ($i == $n - 1) break;
            foreach ($sets[$i] as $k => $_) {
                for ($d = $k - 1; $d <= $k + 1; $d++) {
                    if ($d <= 0) continue;
                    $p = $stones[$i] + $d;
                    if (isset($idx[$p])) {
                        $sets[$idx[$p]][$d] = true;
                    }
                }
            }
            // free memory
            $sets[$i] = [];
        }
        return count($sets[$n - 1]) > 0;
    }
}
```

