This uses patience sorting with binary search, which runs in O(n log n). The array `tails` holds, for each length `k+1`, the smallest possible tail value of an increasing subsequence of that length. For each number, find the first index in `tails` whose value is `>= num` (this gives strictness). Replace that entry with `num`, or append `num` if no such index exists. The final length of `tails` is the answer.

```php
class Solution {

    /**
     * @param Integer[] $nums
     * @return Integer
     */
    function lengthOfLIS($nums) {
        $tails = [];
        $len = 0;
        foreach ($nums as $num) {
            $lo = 0;
            $hi = $len;
            while ($lo < $hi) {
                $mid = intdiv($lo + $hi, 2);
                if ($tails[$mid] < $num) {
                    $lo = $mid + 1;
                } else {
                    $hi = $mid;
                }
            }
            $tails[$lo] = $num;
            if ($lo == $len) {
                $len++;
            }
        }
        return $len;
    }
}
```

