Sort the words by length, then run a DP over a hash map from word to the longest chain ending at that word. For each word, try deleting each character in turn. If the shortened string is in the map, the chain ending at the current word can be that predecessor's chain plus one. Take the best over all deletions, store it, and track the overall maximum.

The examples show trailing spaces inside the strings (an artifact of the problem statement). Since the DP only compares words by deleting characters, a trailing space is treated like any other character, so the logic is unaffected.

Duplicate words are not a concern because words are unique in the original problem. Even with duplicates, the map entry would just be overwritten with the same value.

```php
class Solution {

    /**
     * @param String[] $words
     * @return Integer
     */
    function longestStrChain($words) {
        usort($words, function($a, $b) {
            return strlen($a) - strlen($b);
        });
        $dp = [];
        $best = 1;
        foreach ($words as $w) {
            $cur = 1;
            $n = strlen($w);
            for ($i = 0; $i < $n; $i++) {
                $p = substr($w, 0, $i) . substr($w, $i + 1);
                if (isset($dp[$p]) && $dp[$p] + 1 > $cur) {
                    $cur = $dp[$p] + 1;
                }
            }
            if (!isset($dp[$w]) || $dp[$w] < $cur) {
                $dp[$w] = $cur;
            }
            if ($cur > $best) $best = $cur;
        }
        return $best;
    }
}
```

