Track `cnt[x]`, the number of occurrences of each value, and `freq[c]`, the number of values that occur exactly `c` times. Also track `maxF`, the largest count seen so far.

After processing the prefix of length `i` (1-indexed), the prefix is valid if one of these holds:

1. `maxF == 1`: every element appears once, so removing any one leaves all counts equal to 1 (or the array empty).
2. `freq[maxF] * maxF == i - 1`: one element has count 1 and the rest have count `maxF`. Removing that single element leaves every remaining value at `maxF`. This also covers the case where there is only one distinct value.
3. `freq[maxF] == 1` and `freq[maxF-1] * (maxF-1) + maxF == i`: one value has count `maxF` and all the others have `maxF-1`. Removing one occurrence of the max value equalizes them.

Checking condition 2: if `freq[maxF]*maxF == i-1`, then exactly one element is left over, and it must be a value with count 1. This holds as long as the other values all have count `maxF`.

Checking condition 3: with `freq[maxF] == 1`, the total is `maxF + (maxF-1)*freq[maxF-1]`. If that equals `i`, every other value has count `maxF-1`. When `maxF == 1` this is already covered by condition 1, and `freq[0]` is 0 anyway.

For the update at each element `x`: decrement `freq[cnt[x]]` if `cnt[x] > 0`, increment `cnt[x]`, increment `freq[cnt[x]]`, then set `maxF = max(maxF, cnt[x])`. If any of the conditions holds, set `ans = i`.

Check on example 1, `[2,2,1,1,5,3,3,5]`:
- At `i=7`: counts are 2→2, 1→2, 5→1, 3→2, so `maxF=2` and `freq[2]=3`. Then `3*2 = 6 = i-1`, which is valid, so `ans=7`.
- At `i=8`: counts are 2,2,2,2 with `freq[2]=4`. Then `8 != 7`, and condition 3 fails because `freq[maxF] != 1`. Not valid, so the answer stays 7. ✓.

