Binary search on the running time T. A time T is feasible if the sum of min(b, T) over all batteries is at least n*T. A battery can contribute at most T minutes, since it can only power one computer at a time.

The upper bound is sum/n. With sum up to 1e14, PHP's 64-bit integers handle it. For the check, n*T is at most about 1e14, which is also fine.

Binary search with lo=1 and hi=floor(sum/n):
- mid = (lo+hi+1)>>1
- if feasible(mid), set lo=mid; otherwise set hi=mid-1.

Each check is O(m) and there are about 47 iterations, so the total is roughly 5e6 operations, which is fine.

Since n <= m and every battery is at least 1, sum/n >= 1, so lo=1 is always feasible.

The check can exit early once the accumulated total reaches n*T.

```php
class Solution {

    /**
     * @param Integer $n
     * @param Integer[] $batteries
     * @return Integer
     */
    function maxRunTime($n, $batteries) {
        $sum = array_sum($batteries);
        $lo = 1;
        $hi = intdiv($sum, $n);
        while ($lo < $hi) {
            $mid = ($lo + $hi + 1) >> 1;
            $need = $n * $mid;
            $tot = 0;
            $ok = false;
            foreach ($batteries as $b) {
                $tot += ($b < $mid) ? $b : $mid;
                if ($tot >= $need) {
                    $ok = true;
                    break;
                }
            }
            if ($ok) {
                $lo = $mid;
            } else {
                $hi = $mid - 1;
            }
        }
        return $lo;
    }
}
```

