Approach: cap each garden at `target` and sort ascending. Choose k, the number of gardens made complete, and make them the largest ones. For the remaining smallest gardens, raise the minimum as high as the budget allows, using binary search on a prefix-sum cost.

Details:
- Clamp each value to `min(f, target)` and sort ascending. Let `prefix[i]` be the sum of the first i elements.
- Gardens already at `target` are complete for free. Let `m` be the count of gardens below `target`; these occupy indices `0..m-1`.
- For k from 0 to m, the top k gardens (indices n-k..n-1) are completed. The cost is `k*target - (sum of those k elements)`. If the cost exceeds `newFlowers`, stop, since the cost only grows with k.
- The complete count is `(n-m) + k`. Because the sorted array puts the full gardens at the end, completing the top k gardens includes those already full. So take the top j = number of completed gardens, with cost `j*target - (prefix[n] - prefix[n-j])`, and j runs from `n-m` to n.
- When j = n, all gardens are complete and the beauty is `n*full`.
- Otherwise the remaining gardens are indices `0..r-1` with `r = n-j`, and the budget is `rem = newFlowers - cost`. Find the largest min value `v` in `[0, target-1]` such that raising the first r gardens to at least v costs `≤ rem`.
  - Binary search for the largest index p ≤ r with `a[p-1] < v`. Cost is `p*v - prefix[p]`.
  - Equivalently, find the largest p in `1..r` such that `a[p-1]*p - prefix[p] ≤ rem`. Then `v = min(target-1, floor((rem + prefix[p]) / p))`.
  - This is valid because `a[p-1]*p - prefix[p]` is nondecreasing in p, and v is at least `a[p-1]`. Capping v at `a[r-1]` is unnecessary: if p < r, the next element's cost exceeds rem, so v < a[p]. If p = r, v can exceed `a[r-1]`, which is fine, capped at `target-1`.
  - Since the elements at indices below r are all < target (they are incomplete), `a[0] ≤ target-1`, so p ≥ 1 always works with `cost(1) = 0`.
- Beauty is `j*full + v*partial`. Take the maximum over all j.

PHP integers are 64-bit, and the values here (up to about 1e10 plus 1e10) fit.

For the binary search, define `c(p) = a[p-1]*p - prefix[p]` for p in 1..r. Find the largest p with `c(p) ≤ rem` using lo=1, hi=r.

```php
class Solution {

    /**
     * @param Integer[] $flowers
     * @param Integer $newFlowers
     * @param Integer $target
     * @param Integer $full
     * @param Integer $partial
     * @return Integer
     */
    function maximumBeauty($flowers, $newFlowers, $target, $full, $partial) {
        $n = count($flowers);
        $a = [];
        foreach ($flowers as $f) $a[] = min($f, $target);
        sort($a);
        $prefix = array_fill(0, $n + 1, 0);
        for ($i = 0; $i < $n; $i++) $prefix[$i + 1] = $prefix[$i] + $a[$i];
        $m = 0;
        foreach ($a as $x) if ($x < $target) $m++;
        $best = 0;
        for ($j = $n - $m; $j <= $n; $j++) {
            $cost = $j * $target - ($prefix[$n] - $prefix[$n - $j]);
            if ($cost > $newFlowers) break;
            if ($j == $n) {
                $best = max($best, $n * $full);
                continue;
            }
            $r = $n - $j;
            $rem = $newFlowers - $cost;
            $lo = 1;
            $hi = $r;
            while ($lo < $hi) {
                $mid = intdiv($lo + $hi + 1, 2);
                $c = $a[$mid - 1] * $mid - $prefix[$mid];
                if ($c <= $rem) $lo = $mid;
                else $hi = $mid - 1;
            }
            $p = $lo;
            $v = intdiv($rem + $prefix[$p], $p);
            if ($v > $target - 1) $v = $target - 1;
            $best = max($best, $j * $full + $v * $partial);
        }
        return $best;
    }
}
