The approach is to remove each initial node in turn and count how many nodes end up infected.

With n ≤ 300, a BFS per removal costs O(n²) on the adjacency matrix. Over up to 300 removals that is about 27M operations, which is fine.

Steps:
1. Sort `initial` so that ties on the infected count resolve to the smallest index.
2. For each node `r` in `initial`:
   - Mark `r` as removed.
   - Start a BFS from every other initial node.
   - Count the visited nodes, never entering `r`.
3. Keep the `r` with the smallest count. Since `initial` is sorted and we only replace on a strictly smaller count, ties keep the smallest index.

Each BFS scans the matrix row of every dequeued node, which is O(n²) in total.

```php
class Solution {

    /**
     * @param Integer[][] $graph
     * @param Integer[] $initial
     * @return Integer
     */
    function minMalwareSpread($graph, $initial) {
        $n = count($graph);
        sort($initial);
        $best = -1;
        $bestCnt = PHP_INT_MAX;
        foreach ($initial as $r) {
            $vis = array_fill(0, $n, false);
            $vis[$r] = true;
            $queue = [];
            foreach ($initial as $s) {
                if ($s != $r) {
                    $vis[$s] = true;
                    $queue[] = $s;
                }
            }
            $cnt = count($queue);
            $head = 0;
            while ($head < count($queue)) {
                $u = $queue[$head++];
                $row = $graph[$u];
                for ($v = 0; $v < $n; $v++) {
                    if ($row[$v] == 1 && !$vis[$v]) {
                        $vis[$v] = true;
                        $queue[] = $v;
                        $cnt++;
                    }
                }
            }
            if ($cnt < $bestCnt) {
                $bestCnt = $cnt;
                $best = $r;
            }
        }
        return $best;
    }
}
```

