Count the mismatches against the pattern "0101..." (call it `c0`). The mismatches against "1010..." are then `n - c0`, so the answer is `min(c0, n - c0)`.

The examples in the statement contain trailing spaces (e.g. `"0100 "`), which look like a formatting artifact. To be safe, the code only counts characters that are `'0'` or `'1'`. It keeps a separate index `j` that advances only on those characters, so a stray space cannot shift the pattern or inflate `n`.

For each valid character at position `j`, the expected character in the "0101..." pattern is `j % 2`. If the character differs from that, increment `c0`. Then `n` is the number of valid characters, and the result is `min(c0, n - c0)`.

```php
class Solution {

    /**
     * @param String $s
     * @return Integer
     */
    function minOperations($s) {
        $c0 = 0;
        $n = 0;
        $len = strlen($s);
        for ($i = 0; $i < $len; $i++) {
            $ch = $s[$i];
            if ($ch !== '0' && $ch !== '1') continue;
            $expected = $n % 2;
            if ((int)$ch !== $expected) $c0++;
            $n++;
        }
        return min($c0, $n - $c0);
    }
}
```

