I'll use an iterative BFS, which avoids deep recursion on skewed trees with up to 10^5 nodes. The first leaf reached level by level gives the minimum depth.

If the root is null, the answer is 0. Otherwise, start a queue with the root at depth 1. For each node taken from the queue, if it has no left and no right child, return its depth. Otherwise push its non-null children with depth + 1.

To avoid the O(n) cost of `array_shift`, the queue is a plain array walked with a head index.

```php
class Solution {

    /**
     * @param TreeNode $root
     * @return Integer
     */
    function minDepth($root) {
        if ($root === null) return 0;
        $queue = [[$root, 1]];
        $head = 0;
        while ($head < count($queue)) {
            [$node, $d] = $queue[$head];
            $queue[$head] = null;
            $head++;
            if ($node->left === null && $node->right === null) {
                return $d;
            }
            if ($node->left !== null) $queue[] = [$node->left, $d + 1];
            if ($node->right !== null) $queue[] = [$node->right, $d + 1];
        }
        return 0;
    }
}
```

