Binary search on the number of days. First, if m*k > n, return -1. PHP integers are 64-bit, so m*k (up to about 1e11) is safe.

For a given day d, scan the array and count bouquets greedily. Keep a run of consecutive flowers with bloomDay <= d. Each time the run reaches k, add one bouquet and reset the run to 0. Any flower that has not bloomed also resets the run. If the count reaches m, d is feasible.

The search range is from min(bloomDay) to max(bloomDay). Since m*k <= n, the maximum day is always feasible, so the search always finds an answer.

```php
class Solution {

    /**
     * @param Integer[] $bloomDay
     * @param Integer $m
     * @param Integer $k
     * @return Integer
     */
    function minDays($bloomDay, $m, $k) {
        $n = count($bloomDay);
        if ($m * $k > $n) return -1;
        $lo = min($bloomDay);
        $hi = max($bloomDay);
        while ($lo < $hi) {
            $mid = intdiv($lo + $hi, 2);
            $cnt = 0;
            $run = 0;
            foreach ($bloomDay as $b) {
                if ($b <= $mid) {
                    $run++;
                    if ($run == $k) {
                        $cnt++;
                        $run = 0;
                    }
                } else {
                    $run = 0;
                }
            }
            if ($cnt >= $m) {
                $hi = $mid;
            } else {
                $lo = $mid + 1;
            }
        }
        return $lo;
    }
}
