inference_trace:
This is a bitmask DP over subsets of tasks.

Let `dp[mask]` be a pair (sessions, time used in the last session), minimized lexicographically. Start with `dp[0] = (1, 0)`. For each mask and each task `i` not in it:
- If `time + tasks[i] <= sessionTime`, the new state is `(sessions, time + tasks[i])`.
- Otherwise the new state is `(sessions + 1, tasks[i])`.

Keep the lexicographically smaller pair for `mask | (1 << i)`. The answer is `dp[full].sessions`.

To compare pairs cheaply, encode each state as a single integer `sessions * 100 + time`. Time is at most 15, so this keeps the ordering correct. The initial state is 100. For a transition from value `v`, let `s = intdiv(v, 100)` and `t = v % 100`.

Complexity is 2^14 × 14, which is small.

```php
class Solution {

    /**
     * @param Integer[] $tasks
     * @param Integer $sessionTime
     * @return Integer
     */
    function minSessions($tasks, $sessionTime) {
        $n = count($tasks);
        $full = (1 << $n) - 1;
        $INF = PHP_INT_MAX;
        $dp = array_fill(0, $full + 1, $INF);
        $dp[0] = 100;
        for ($mask = 0; $mask < $full; $mask++) {
            $v = $dp[$mask];
            if ($v === $INF) continue;
            $s = intdiv($v, 100);
            $t = $v % 100;
            for ($i = 0; $i < $n; $i++) {
                if ($mask & (1 << $i)) continue;
                if ($t + $tasks[$i] <= $sessionTime) {
                    $nv = $s * 100 + $t + $tasks[$i];
                } else {
                    $nv = ($s + 1) * 100 + $tasks[$i];
                }
                $nm = $mask | (1 << $i);
                if ($nv < $dp[$nm]) $dp[$nm] = $nv;
            }
        }
        return intdiv($dp[$full], 100);
    }
}
```

