inference_trace:
The count is defined recursively. For an array, the first element is the root. The remaining elements split into those smaller than the root (left) and those larger (right), each keeping its original order. The number of orderings is C(l+r, l) · f(left) · f(right). The answer is that value minus 1, because the count includes the original ordering.

Binomials are computed with Pascal's triangle mod p, which is 1001×1001 and fast enough. With n ≤ 1000 the recursion depth is at most 1000, which is fine for PHP, and the total work is O(n²) in the worst case.

Define `f(arr)`: if count ≤ 2, return 1. Otherwise take the root, split into left and right, and return C(n-1, |left|) · f(left) · f(right) mod p. The final answer is (f(nums) - 1 + p) % p.

Products reach about 1e18, which fits in PHP's 64-bit integers (max about 9.2e18) because each factor is below 1e9+7. So `a*b` is at most about 1e18 and safe.

The Pascal table is built once per call as a local array of arrays. The recursion is written as a private method that takes the table. Example 3, [1,2,3]: the root is 1, left is empty, right is [2,3], so C(2,0)·f([2,3]) = 1, giving 1 - 1 = 0. ✓.

```php
class Solution {

    private $C = [];
    private $MOD = 1000000007;

    /**
     * @param Integer[] $nums
     * @return Integer
     */
    function numOfWays($nums) {
        $n = count($nums);
        $MOD = $this->MOD;
        $this->C = [];
        for ($i = 0; $i <= $n; $i++) {
            $this->C[$i] = array_fill(0, $i + 1, 0);
            $this->C[$i][0] = 1;
            $this->C[$i][$i] = 1;
            for ($j = 1; $j < $i; $j++) {
                $this->C[$i][$j] = ($this->C[$i-1][$j-1] + $this->C[$i-1][$j]) % $MOD;
            }
        }
        $r = $this->f($nums);
        return ($r - 1 + $MOD) % $MOD;
    }

    private function f($arr) {
        $n = count($arr);
        if ($n <= 2) return 1;
        $root = $arr[0];
        $left = [];
        $right = [];
        for ($i = 1; $i < $n; $i++) {
            if ($arr[$i] < $root) $left[] = $arr[$i];
            else $right[] = $arr[$i];
        }
        $l = count($left);
        $res = $this->C[$n - 1][$l];
        $res = ($res * $this->f($left)) % $this->MOD;
        $res = ($res * $this->f($right)) % $this->MOD;
        return $res;
    }
}
```

