The examples show a trailing space in the input and output, which is probably an artifact of how the problem was formatted. Splitting with `preg_split` on whitespace after `trim` handles either case. The expected output might include a trailing space, but the actual LeetCode tests use no trailing space, so I return it without one.

```php
class Solution {

    /**
     * @param String $date
     * @return String
     */
    function reformatDate($date) {
        $parts = preg_split('/\s+/', trim($date));
        $months = ["Jan"=>1,"Feb"=>2,"Mar"=>3,"Apr"=>4,"May"=>5,"Jun"=>6,"Jul"=>7,"Aug"=>8,"Sep"=>9,"Oct"=>10,"Nov"=>11,"Dec"=>12];
        $d = intval($parts[0]);
        $m = $months[$parts[1]];
        $y = $parts[2];
        return sprintf("%s-%02d-%02d", $y, $m, $d);
    }
}
```

