This is a standard DP problem. Let `dp[i][j]` be true when the first `i` characters of `s` match the first `j` characters of `p`.

- Base case: `dp[0][0] = true`.
- For `j` from 1 to `n`, if `p[j-1]` is `'*'`, then `dp[0][j] = dp[0][j-2]`, since the starred element can match zero characters.
- For `i` from 0 to `m` and `j` from 1 to `n`:
  - If `p[j-1] == '*'`, the starred element can match zero occurrences, so `dp[i][j] = dp[i][j-2]`. It can also match one more character when `i > 0` and `p[j-2]` is `'.'` or equals `s[i-1]`, which gives `dp[i][j] |= dp[i-1][j]`.
  - Otherwise, if `i > 0` and `p[j-1]` is `'.'` or equals `s[i-1]`, then `dp[i][j] = dp[i-1][j-1]`.

The examples in the statement contain trailing spaces, which look like formatting artifacts, so I treat the strings as given. The pattern is guaranteed to have a valid character before every `*`, so `j-2` is always in range when `p[j-1]` is `'*'`.

```php
class Solution {

    /**
     * @param String $s
     * @param String $p
     * @return Boolean
     */
    function isMatch($s, $p) {
        $m = strlen($s);
        $n = strlen($p);
        $dp = array_fill(0, $m + 1, array_fill(0, $n + 1, false));
        $dp[0][0] = true;
        for ($i = 0; $i <= $m; $i++) {
            for ($j = 1; $j <= $n; $j++) {
                if ($p[$j - 1] === '*') {
                    if ($j >= 2) {
                        $v = $dp[$i][$j - 2];
                        if (!$v && $i > 0 && ($p[$j - 2] === '.' || $p[$j - 2] === $s[$i - 1])) {
                            $v = $dp[$i - 1][$j];
                        }
                        $dp[$i][$j] = $v;
                    }
                } else {
                    if ($i > 0 && ($p[$j - 1] === '.' || $p[$j - 1] === $s[$i - 1])) {
                        $dp[$i][$j] = $dp[$i - 1][$j - 1];
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        return $dp[$m][$n];
    }
}
