If the string is empty the answer is 0. If it is a palindrome, one step removes it all. Otherwise two steps suffice: remove every `a` (a palindrome, since all characters are equal), then every `b`.

The examples in the statement contain a trailing space (e.g. `"ababa "`), which is probably a formatting artifact. If the real input did contain a trailing space, the palindrome check would behave differently. The constraints say only `'a'` and `'b'` appear, so I'll apply the check to the string as given, without trimming.

```php
class Solution {

    /**
     * @param String $s
     * @return Integer
     */
    function removePalindromeSub($s) {
        if ($s === "") return 0;
        return $s === strrev($s) ? 1 : 2;
    }
}
```

