Build an adjacency map from the pairs. In the original array, the two end elements each appear in exactly one pair, so any element with a single neighbor is an endpoint. Start from one endpoint and walk along the chain, never stepping back to the previous element.

PHP array keys with negative integers work fine. Each adjacency list holds at most 2 entries.

Steps:
1. Build `$adj[$u][] = $v` and `$adj[$v][] = $u` for every pair.
2. Find a key whose list has exactly one neighbor and use it as the start. Keys from `foreach` are ints, which is fine.
3. Set `$prev` to a sentinel that can't be a valid value (null), and `$cur` to the start.
4. Loop n times (n = count of pairs + 1). Append `$cur` to the result, then pick the neighbor that isn't `$prev`.
   - For the start, the only neighbor is chosen, since `$prev` is null.
   - For middle nodes, one of the two neighbors is `$prev`, so the other is chosen.
   - For the last node, its only neighbor is `$prev`, so there is no next node and the loop ends.

Since elements are unique, comparing a neighbor to `$prev` by value is safe.

```php
class Solution {

    /**
     * @param Integer[][] $adjacentPairs
     * @return Integer[]
     */
    function restoreArray($adjacentPairs) {
        $adj = [];
        foreach ($adjacentPairs as $p) {
            $adj[$p[0]][] = $p[1];
            $adj[$p[1]][] = $p[0];
        }
        $start = null;
        foreach ($adj as $k => $list) {
            if (count($list) == 1) {
                $start = $k;
                break;
            }
        }
        $n = count($adjacentPairs) + 1;
        $res = [];
        $prev = null;
        $cur = $start;
        for ($i = 0; $i < $n; $i++) {
            $res[] = $cur;
            $next = null;
            foreach ($adj[$cur] as $nb) {
                if ($prev === null || $nb !== $prev) {
                    $next = $nb;
                    break;
                }
            }
            $prev = $cur;
            $cur = $next;
            if ($cur === null) break;
        }
        return $res;
    }
}
```

