Sort the products, then for each prefix length of `searchWord`, keep a narrowing candidate list. Since the products are sorted, the matches for each longer prefix are a subset of the matches for the shorter one, in the same order. So I filter the previous list by whether each product starts with the current prefix and take the first three.

The constraints are small enough for this: at most 1000 products and a search word of at most 1000 characters. Each filter pass costs at most 1000 × prefix-length comparisons, and the filtered list only shrinks.

To compare, I check `$p[$i-1] === $searchWord[$i-1]` against the previous list, which is already filtered to match the first `i-1` characters. A product matches at step `i` if its length is at least `i` and its character at position `i-1` equals the search word's. That makes each step O(candidates).

Use `sort($products)`, which compares strings lexicographically. The values are lowercase letters, so the default flags are fine. The examples show trailing spaces, but those are just formatting artifacts.

```php
class Solution {

    /**
     * @param String[] $products
     * @param String $searchWord
     * @return String[][]
     */
    function suggestedProducts($products, $searchWord) {
        sort($products, SORT_STRING);
        $cand = $products;
        $res = [];
        $n = strlen($searchWord);
        for ($i = 1; $i <= $n; $i++) {
            $c = $searchWord[$i - 1];
            $next = [];
            foreach ($cand as $p) {
                if (strlen($p) >= $i && $p[$i - 1] === $c) {
                    $next[] = $p;
                }
            }
            $cand = $next;
