Scan the key presses once, tracking the longest duration seen so far and the key that produced it. For each press, the duration is `releaseTimes[i] - releaseTimes[i-1]`, or just `releaseTimes[0]` for the first press. If a duration beats the current maximum, or ties it with a lexicographically larger key, update the answer. Since the keys are single lowercase letters, comparing them as strings works for the tie-break.

```php
class Solution {

    /**
     * @param Integer[] $releaseTimes
     * @param String $keysPressed
     * @return String
     */
    function slowestKey($releaseTimes, $keysPressed) {
        $n = count($releaseTimes);
        $maxD = -1;
        $best = '';
        $prev = 0;
        for ($i = 0; $i < $n; $i++) {
            $d = $releaseTimes[$i] - $prev;
            $prev = $releaseTimes[$i];
            $k = $keysPressed[$i];
            if ($d > $maxD || ($d == $maxD && strcmp($k, $best) > 0)) {
                $maxD = $d;
                $best = $k;
            }
        }
        return $best;
    }
}
```

