inference_trace:
Approach: compute each node's in-degree. A valid tree has exactly one root (in-degree 0), and every other node has in-degree exactly 1. Then run a BFS from the root and check that all n nodes are visited.

Steps:
1. Count in-degrees from `leftChild` and `rightChild`. If any node's in-degree exceeds 1, return false.
2. Find nodes with in-degree 0. If there isn't exactly one, return false.
3. BFS from that root, counting visited nodes. A visited set guards against cycles, though in-degree ≤ 1 with a single root already rules out most of them.
4. Return true only if the visited count equals n.

```php
class Solution {

    /**
     * @param Integer $n
     * @param Integer[] $leftChild
     * @param Integer[] $rightChild
     * @return Boolean
     */
    function validateBinaryTreeNodes($n, $leftChild, $rightChild) {
        $indeg = array_fill(0, $n, 0);
        for ($i = 0; $i < $n; $i++) {
            foreach ([$leftChild[$i], $rightChild[$i]] as $c) {
                if ($c != -1) {
                    $indeg[$c]++;
                    if ($indeg[$c] > 1) return false;
                }
            }
        }
        $root = -1;
        for ($i = 0; $i < $n; $i++) {
            if ($indeg[$i] == 0) {
                if ($root != -1) return false;
                $root = $i;
            }
        }
        if ($root == -1) return false;
        $visited = array_fill(0, $n, false);
        $visited[$root] = true;
        $queue = [$root];
        $head = 0;
        $count = 1;
        while ($head < count($queue)) {
            $u = $queue[$head++];
            foreach ([$leftChild[$u], $rightChild[$u]] as $c) {
                if ($c != -1) {
                    if ($visited[$c]) return false;
                    $visited[$c] = true;
                    $count++;
                    $queue[] = $c;
                }
            }
        }
        return $count == $n;
    }
}
```

