This uses the standard greedy two-pointer approach with backtracking to the most recent `*`, which runs in O(n·m) time in the worst case and O(1) extra space.

1. Walk through `s` with index `i` and through `p` with index `j`. Keep `star = -1` as the position of the last `*` seen in `p`, and `mark = 0` as the position in `s` where that `*` currently stops matching.
2. If `p[j]` is `?` or equals `s[i]`, advance both pointers.
3. If `p[j]` is `*`, record `star = j` and `mark = i`, then advance `j` only. This first tries the `*` matching the empty sequence.
4. If there is no match but a previous `*` exists, backtrack: set `j = star + 1`, increment `mark`, and set `i = mark`. This makes the `*` absorb one more character of `s`.
5. If there is no match and no previous `*`, return false.
6. After `s` is consumed, skip any remaining `*` in `p`. The match succeeds if `j` has reached the end of `p`.

```php
class Solution {

    /**
     * @param String $s
     * @param String $p
     * @return Boolean
     */
    function isMatch($s, $p) {
        $n = strlen($s);
        $m = strlen($p);
        $i = 0;
        $j = 0;
        $star = -1;
        $mark = 0;
        while ($i < $n) {
            if ($j < $m && ($p[$j] === '?' || $p[$j] === $s[$i])) {
                $i++;
                $j++;
            } elseif ($j < $m && $p[$j] === '*') {
                $star = $j;
                $mark = $i;
                $j++;
            } elseif ($star !== -1) {
                $j = $star + 1;
                $mark++;
                $i = $mark;
            } else {
                return false;
            }
        }
        while ($j < $m && $p[$j] === '*') {
            $j++;
        }
        return $j === $m;
    }
}
